\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsthm,booktabs,hyperref}
\setlength{\emergencystretch}{2em}
\newtheorem{theorem}{Theorem}
\newtheorem{lemma}{Lemma}
\title{Two minima among cubic graphs of girth nine}
\author{Human Compression project: a component of the F012 recovery}
\date{}
\begin{document}\maketitle

All graphs in this note are finite, simple and connected. An
edge-girth-regular graph with parameters $(n,k,g,\lambda)$ is a
$k$-regular graph on $n$ vertices, of girth $g$, in which each edge
belongs to exactly $\lambda$ cycles of length $g$. Write
$n(k,g,\lambda)$ for the least possible order. We recover the two
values established by Goedgebeur and Jooken in \cite{gj}:
\[
                    n(3,9,6)=n(3,9,8)=60.
\]
The exhaustive classification used below is the earlier theorem of
Brinkmann, McKay and Saager \cite{bms}: a cubic graph of girth nine
has at least $58$ vertices, and their Appendix lists all eighteen
isomorphism classes at order $58$. We retain that theorem as an
external premise; the present computation checks its listed graphs
and does not repeat the classification.

\begin{lemma}\label{count}
If a $k$-regular graph on $n$ vertices is edge-girth-regular with
girth $g$ and parameter $\lambda$, its number $c_g$ of cycles of
length $g$ satisfies
\[
                         2g c_g=nk\lambda.
\]
Consequently, a cubic edge-girth-regular graph of order $58$ and
girth nine has $29\mid c_9$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Count pairs consisting of an edge and a cycle of length $g$
containing it. There are $g c_g$ such pairs by counting cycles,
and $(nk/2)\lambda$ by counting edges. For $n=58$, $k=3$ and
$g=9$ the equation becomes $3c_9=29\lambda$. Since $29$ is
coprime to $3$, it divides $c_9$.
\end{proof}

In the labelling of \cite{bms}, each graph $G_i$ consists of the
Hamiltonian cycle $1,2,\ldots,58,1$ together with a listed perfect
matching. The exact matchings are retained in \texttt{cages58.json}.
The following table gives the independently computed number $c_9$,
and the minimum and maximum numbers of nine-cycles through an edge.
Every total agrees with the total printed in the original Appendix.
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{rrrrrrrr}
\toprule
$i$&$c_9$&minimum&maximum&$i$&$c_9$&minimum&maximum\\
\midrule
1&80&6&12&10&73&5&11\\
2&97&8&12&11&75&5&12\\
3&100&10&12&12&95&8&12\\
4&84&6&13&13&95&7&12\\
5&84&6&13&14&96&8&12\\
6&86&6&14&15&92&4&12\\
7&93&7&13&16&70&5&12\\
8&95&8&12&17&70&5&10\\
9&96&8&13&18&92&6&11\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
None of the totals is divisible by $29$. Thus none of the eighteen
graphs is edge-girth-regular, by Lemma~\ref{count}. The unequal
minimum and maximum entries provide a second direct check of the
same conclusion. For the particular value $\lambda=8$, integrality
already excludes order $58$, since $3c_9=232$ has no integer
solution. For $\lambda=6$ it would require $c_9=58$, also absent
from the table.

\begin{theorem}
The equalities $n(3,9,6)=n(3,9,8)=60$ hold.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The acquired cage theorem excludes every order below $58$.
The table and Lemma~\ref{count} exclude order $58$. A cubic graph
has even order, since its degree sum $3n$ equals twice the number
of edges. Hence both required minima are at least $60$.

For the upper bounds, retain the two graphs at lines $416$ and
$417$ of the authors' graph6 data file, at repository commit
\texttt{41022f4a3ad84e4502f8889c5b64132225411ca2}.
Their exact encodings and original line hashes are recorded in
\texttt{witnesses60.json}. Direct verification gives connected
cubic graphs on $60$ vertices with girth nine. In the first, all
$90$ edges lie on exactly six nine-cycles; in the second, all
$90$ edges lie on exactly eight. They have respectively $60$
and $80$ nine-cycles. Thus they give the required upper bounds.
\end{proof}

\paragraph{Verification of the finite data.}
The retained Python checker reconstructs every $58$-vertex graph
from its Hamiltonian cycle and matching, and decodes the two
graph6 witnesses directly. It checks simplicity, connectedness
and cubic degree. For each edge $uv$, breadth-first search in
$G-uv$ computes the shortest $u$--$v$ path. The minimum of these
distances plus one is the girth, which equals nine in all twenty
graphs.

For a fixed orientation $u,v$ of an edge, the checker enumerates
all simple $u$--$v$ paths of length eight in $G-uv$. Adding the
deleted edge gives a bijection with the nine-cycles containing
that edge. As a separate check it enumerates simple cycles from
their least vertex and retains precisely the direction in which
the second vertex is smaller than the last. Every undirected
nine-cycle has exactly one such representation. These traversals
agree on the count at every edge of every graph. The full edge
counts and cycle totals are retained in \texttt{author\_report.json}.
All counts use exact integers; the computation is a finite check
of the supplied graphs, not an exhaustive generation theorem.

\paragraph{Attribution and scope.}
The two minima and their positive witnesses are due to Goedgebeur
and Jooken. The lower-order exclusion and completeness of the
eighteen-graph list are due to Brinkmann, McKay and Saager.
The incidence argument is elementary and carries no priority
claim. Our contribution here is an explicit short proof interface,
faithful acquisition of the original graph data, and a separately
replayable verification by two cycle-counting traversals. The
repository edition of the positive data is later than the fixed
June 2024 manuscript; only the verified existence of these two
graphs is used. This note makes no claim about the completeness
or number of extremal graphs at order $60$, the other F012 table
entries, or the whole original.

